non-breaking $HOME user directories to external drive:

why:

- non-breaking changes: If you move you entire $HOME directory to external drive/partition, if the external brokes (or not present), linux will not boot.
- $HOME directories are symbolic links to whatever directories you point them to
- no need for hide anything with dots .beforeTheName nor .hidden file hacks
- tested on ubuntu / mint

====

create in $HOME/.config/ a user-dirs.conf file and edit with the following entry:

[UserDirs]
enabled=False

this will prevent any system overwrite of the $HOME/.config/user-dirs.dirs file to default state

check that $HOME/.config/user-dirs.dirs file contents are:

XDG_DESKTOP_DIR="$HOME/Desktop"
XDG_DOCUMENTS_DIR="$HOME/Documents"
XDG_DOWNLOAD_DIR="$HOME/Downloads"
XDG_MUSIC_DIR="$HOME/Music"
XDG_PICTURES_DIR="$HOME/Pictures"
XDG_PUBLICSHARE_DIR="$HOME/Public"
XDG_REPOSITORIES_DIR="$HOME/Repositories"
XDG_TEMPLATES_DIR="$HOME/Templates"
XDG_VIDEOS_DIR="$HOME/Videos"

edit permissions of file to readonly (just in case):

chmod 444 ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs

====

in your $HOME directories, change the user directories to symbolic links, pointing whenever you want them to be (generally an external drive)

you external drive is always mounted on: /media/userName/diskName

- delete one of the directories 
- create a symbolic link with the same name, pointing to the external directories with the following command:

!! BE SURE YOU SAVED STORED INFORMATION INSIDE HOME/DIRECTORIES BEFORE DELETING !!

rm -r ~/Desktop
ln -s /media/userName/diskName/Desktop ~/Desktop

first argument on the second command is the route where the destination /directories are mounted on the filesystem, second argument is the origin /directory

(find handy bash script to automate task here)
https://github.com/inertiqa/dot-files/blob/master/homeMigration.sh

do it for all user generated directories and reboot, now every user generated directories inside $HOME/ are symbolic links to the external drive directories with the same names

- all commands on user directories works as expected
- if you delete/lose linux, all userdata will be saved (are stored on another drive)
- if the external drive is not present/broken, linux will normally boot into the user (but with broken $HOME/userDirectories links)

be happy
